@startuml UC1_Login_SignUp_Sequence_Diagram

title UC1: Login / Sign Up - Sequence Diagram

actor "End User" as User
participant "Frontend" as Frontend
participant "Authentication Service\n(Firebase Auth)" as Auth

User -> Frontend: Open application, navigate to\nany page, or resume after a while
Frontend -> Frontend: Check local persistence\nfor cached refresh token

note right of Frontend
  Route guard (A4): this same check runs
  on every page, not just app launch. Any
  page other than the login form requires
  a valid session - a deep link, a direct
  URL, or a session expiring mid-use all
  land back on this check.
end note

alt Session restored (refresh token cached)
  alt Cached ID token still valid (< ~1hr old)
    Frontend -> Frontend: Reuse cached ID token
  else Cached ID token expired
    Frontend -> Auth: Silently refresh using\ncached refresh token\n(no password re-entry)
    Auth --> Frontend: New Firebase ID token
  end
else No session restored (no cached refresh token)
  Frontend -> User: Display login form

  opt A1: New Account
    User -> Frontend: Select "Register" +\nusername/email/password
    Frontend -> Auth: Create account
    Auth --> Frontend: Account created
  end

  opt A3: Forgot Password
    User -> Frontend: Select "Forgot Password"
    Frontend -> Auth: Request password reset
    Auth --> Frontend: Reset email sent
    note right: Flow ends (A3)
  end

  User -> Frontend: Enter credentials

  alt E1: Authentication Service unavailable
    Frontend -> Auth: Sign in
    Auth --> Frontend: Service unavailable
    Frontend -> User: Display maintenance message
    note right: Flow ends (E1)
  else Authentication Service available
    Frontend -> Auth: Sign in with credentials

    alt A2: Invalid credentials
      Auth --> Frontend: Error
      Frontend -> User: Display error,\nre-prompt for credentials
      note right: Flow ends (A2)
    else Credentials valid
      Auth --> Frontend: Firebase ID token +\nrefresh token
      Frontend -> Frontend: Cache refresh token\nin local persistence
    end
  end
end

note over Frontend
  Frontend now holds a valid Firebase ID
  token and an active session. Continues
  into Create Profile (UC2) on its first
  authenticated call to the User Actions
  Server (see UC2 sequence diagram) -
  no backend involvement in UC1 itself.
  Only the ID token is ever sent to the
  User Actions Server; the refresh token
  stays cached client-side and is only
  ever shown to the Authentication Service.
end note

@enduml
